think Lindy-first crux-first: the crypto library serves every module that signs or verifies — highest-Lindy new thread. Crux hunt found a real gap: the AEAD rung proved only the IETF 12-byte-nonce path (crypto_aead_init_ietf); Monocypher's FLAGSHIP crypto_aead_lock is XChaCha20-Poly1305 (24-byte nonce, HChaCha20 subkey), the API Lotus/Vault/Comlink actually call, and it had NO Monocypher-source parity — only crypto/xchacha20.rye self-test
think crypto/xchacha20.rye already implements exactly the crypto_aead_lock construction (HChaCha20 subkey + RFC 8439 AEAD, draft-irtf-cfrg-xchacha §2.3); mirrored the proven parity pattern: C harness over the unmodified vendored source, Rye printing harness over the GREEN module, Rishi witness diffing byte-for-byte and anchoring to a published KAT
think Reused the published known-answer already in xchacha20.rye's selftest (Zig std.crypto neutral vector: key 0x45x32, nonce 0x2ax24, aad 'Additional data', RFC 114-byte plaintext) so the anchor is a real second implementation, never a recited constant
obs GREEN on the first full pass — Monocypher's crypto_aead_lock and our crypto/xchacha20.rye produce byte-identical ciphertext (994d2dd3…bde9aaf) and tag (5a94b8f2…e234), both matching the published KAT
obs Count guard untouched: added only tools/ files plus one tools/xchacha20.rye symlink, so crypto/*.rye stays 56 (guard GREEN). Gate-free and purely local — published test vector, no real key, no signature, no network, no funds, no device
